A root component's state, mounted on two windows, on every target that renders
them. This is the fixture that pins the per-platform difference, because the
difference is the documented behavior rather than an accident.

`main()` at the root of the file renders the component, and the inliner
substitutes its body into the package body -- so the one `hits__inst0` the
splice named is the var both windows read, and what *that* means is the
platform's answer. A target whose windows are one
process shares one cell: fyne and gtk4 each put `hits` in one state struct
(fyne's Model, gtk4's `snglState`), and the click in window A is read by
window B. html is one of them now: A is the
document and B a `<dialog>` in it, so the page holds one `state` and the click
patches both labels. It used to write B as a second document, b.html, with a
`state` of its own -- and A's click patched `__n1`, B's label, in a document
that did not hold it, so the handler threw on a null element. Pages are where
html still copies state, each page's document being one of its own. Neither
answer is imposed by the lowering -- forcing either would be the language
overriding the platform it compiled to -- and the author picks a target
knowing it. Top-level
state shared across windows is a performance tool, not the default way to write
an application.

The sharp edge is the shared pointer reaching one state struct through two
owners. It
must emit one field: CodegenCtx.ModelState dedupes by the *ir.Var, and without
that the Go targets declared `hits int` twice in the same struct and did not
compile.

`bump` reaches no target at all, and that is the right answer rather than a
gap: it is pure, `passInlinePure` folds its one call, and the shake then has a
declaration nothing names. It used to be emitted because `ir.Window.Funcs` was
a shake root, so the hoist kept alive a func the program had already finished
with. root_component_mutating_func.txtar is where a root component's func
survives to every backend.

`__inst0` throughout is that splice, and it is the whole of what has replaced
the lift: nothing scans the declarations for the root family any more, so a
root component nobody instantiates renders nothing and declares nothing.

Reading a var owned by several windows once needed a scope of its own
(CodegenCtx.sharedWindowScope): a multi-window program's single Model was
scoped to no one window, rightly -- two windows' `count` are two names -- but
a declaration owned by *all* of them is one *ir.Var reachable from each, so
scoping to it chooses nothing. Without that, every read here rendered as a
bare `hits` the emitted Go never declared, standing beside the Model field it
should have projected onto.

bubbletea and android show one window, and refuse a second where it is
written (cmd/sngl/testdata/window_second_refused.txt), so neither builds this
program; both used to render the first window and drop the second without a
word. fyne and gtk4 build both, as two OS windows over the one state
(window_several.txtar).

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `(?sm)^\thits\s+int$.*^\thits\s+int$` -- one Model, one cell: the var is mounted on both windows and must be declared once
deny * `(?i)\bbump\b` -- a pure func whose only call was folded reaches no backend; the hoist used to keep it alive as a shake root
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `[(,+]hits\b` -- a read of the shared var is `m.hits`; the bare `Sprint(hits)` is the scope gap this fixture is for
deny out/none/html/index.html `var state = \{[^}]*\};[\s\S]*var state = ` -- one document, one state: B is a dialog in A's page
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main root {
    var hits = 0

    func bump(n int) => n + 1

    ui.window #a(title="A") {
        ui.text(value="a {hits}")
        ui.button(text="go", @click { hits = bump(hits) })
    }

    ui.window #b(title="B") {
        ui.text(value="b {hits}")
    }
}

main()
